<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banda Singh Bahadur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1670">1670</a>–<a href="/years/1716">1716</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banda Singh Bahadur was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> military leader who, after meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> in 1708, led a rising against Mughal rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a> in 1710, set up a Sikh administration with its own coinage and abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> estates in the areas he held. Besieged and captured at Gurdas Nangal, he was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1716.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banda-singh-bahadur.jpg" alt="Banda Singh Bahadur" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Possible_depiction_of_Banda_Singh_Bahadur_from_an_illustrated_folio_of_%E2%80%98Tawarikh-i_Jahandar_Shah%E2%80%99,_Awadh_or_Lucknow,_ca.1770.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Lachman Dev on 27 October 1670 at Rajouri, in the poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> family of a farmer, Ram Dev. Sources from his lifetime, such as the Akhbar-i-Darbar-i-Mualla and the Sri Gur Sobha, do not name his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>; much later tradition calls him a Dogra Rajput or a Sodhi Khatri. A tale holds that at 15 he was so moved by a doe he killed while hunting that he became an ascetic. The ascetic Janaki Prasad named him Madho Das, and as a bairagi of the Vaishnavite tradition he founded a monastery at Nanded on the Godavari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a>'s visit in 1708 he took Amrit and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>. In September 1708 the Guru gave him the title Bahadur, a war drum, the Nishan Sahib, five arrows and letters calling on all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> to join him. He won support in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> by suppressing bandits and wrote to the Sikh communities of Majha, Doaba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. His first major action was the sack of the Mughal provincial capital of Samana in November 1709.</p><p>Against Wazir Khan, Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a>, whose army of more than 20,000 had artillery and elephants, his forces had only spears, arrows and swords. He made the village of Mukhlisgarh his capital, renamed it Lohgarh, and struck coins there, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> forces took Saharanpur and Muzaffarnagar. Emperor Bahadur Shah I marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and on 29 August 1710 ordered all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> to shave their beards so that no Sikh agents could enter his camps. A Mughal army under Munim Khan retook Sirhind and besieged Lohgarh with 60,000 troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1714 Emperor Farrukh Siyar had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a>'s widow Mata Sundari urge Banda to submit. He refused, she excommunicated him, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> split into the Tat Khalsa and his own Bandais, divided over his red robes, his insistence on vegetarianism and his slogan Fateh Darshan. About fifteen thousand followers left him. In March 1715 Abd al-Samad Khan, Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, besieged him at Gurdas Nangal, and on 7 December 1715 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> broke into the starving garrison.</p><p>He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> with 780 prisoners, who were executed at about 100 a day. His four-year-old son Ajai Singh was killed before him, and on 9 June 1716 he was tortured and beheaded. A British East India Company diplomat's letter on the executions is one of the earliest Western accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>. The 328-foot Fateh Burj at the site of the Battle of Chappar Chiri is dedicated to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banda Singh Bahadur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death <b>Banda Singh Bahadur</b> led a peasant rebellion that briefly ruled parts of the Punjab before he was captured and executed in Delhi in 1716.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>After the execution of <b>Banda Singh Bahadur</b> in 1716, Sikh fighters organised in bands during decades of persecution by Mughal governors and invasions by Ahmad Shah Durrani.</blockquote><a href="/state/sikh-misls">Sikh Misls</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rajauri, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.38,74.3&amp;year=1699" aria-label="Rajauri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Banda Singh Bahadur"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6011 4648h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6011 4648h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.38,74.3&amp;year=1699">What was here in 1699? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
